<body>

<div id="progress" aria-hidden="true"></div>

<header class="topbar">
  <div class="brand">☀️ Why Is the Sky Blue? <span class="zh">天空为什么是蓝色的？</span></div>
  <nav aria-label="Sections">
    <a href="#s1">1 · Toddler 🧸</a>
    <a href="#s2">2 · High School 🎓</a>
    <a href="#s3">3 · Physics PhD 🧪</a>
  </nav>
  <div class="controls">
    <button id="fontMinus" type="button" title="Decrease text size" aria-label="Decrease text size">A−</button>
    <button id="fontPlus" type="button" title="Increase text size" aria-label="Increase text size">A+</button>
  </div>
</header>

<main>
  <p class="intro">
    Same physics, three zoom levels — English and Simplified Chinese, side by side.<br>
    同一套物理，三种“缩放层级”——英文与简体中文对照呈现。
  </p>

  <!-- ================= SECTION 1 : TODDLER ================= -->
  <section class="block" id="s1" aria-labelledby="s1-title">
    <div class="block-head">
      <span class="num" aria-hidden="true">1</span>
      <h2 id="s1-title">For a 3-Year-Old <span class="zh-h">给 3 岁的小朋友</span> <span class="emo">🧸</span></h2>
    </div>
    <div class="panels">
      <article class="panel en">
        <span class="chip en">EN · English</span>
        <p class="lede">The sun's light looks white, but inside it, there's a whole rainbow hiding — blue, red, yellow, all the colors!</p>
        <p>When the sunlight bumps into the tiny, tiny bits of air all around us, the air plays a funny trick: it mostly bounces the <strong>blue</strong> light around, all over the sky, like glitter.</p>
        <p>So when you look up, the air is sharing all that blue light with your eyes — and the sky looks blue!</p>
        <p>The red and orange light don't bounce much, so they keep walking straight. That's why at sunset, when the light has to walk through <em>a whole lot</em> of air, all the blue has bounced away and you get to see the orange.</p>
        <p>The sky is blue because the air loves bouncing blue light, and it's bouncing some of it straight to you right now. Look up and catch it! ☁️</p>
      </article>
      <article class="panel zh" lang="zh-Hans">
        <span class="chip zh">中文 · Simplified Chinese</span>
        <p class="lede">太阳光看起来是白色的，可它里面藏着一条完整的彩虹——蓝色、红色、黄色，什么颜色都有！</p>
        <p>当阳光撞到环绕我们身边的、非常非常小的空气微粒时，空气会玩一个有趣的小把戏：它把<strong>蓝色</strong>的光弹得到处都是，整个天上都是，就像亮晶晶的闪光粉一样。</p>
        <p>所以当你抬头看的时候，空气正把那些蓝色的光分给你的眼睛——天空看起来就是蓝色的啦！</p>
        <p>红色和橙色的光不怎么被弹，它们一直直线往前走。这就是为什么日落的时候，阳光要穿过<em>好多好多</em>的空气，所有的蓝色都已经被弹走了，你看到的就是橙色了。</p>
        <p>天空是蓝色的，因为空气特别喜欢弹蓝色的光，而且它现在正把其中一些光直直地弹向你呢。抬起头，接住它吧！☁️</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ================= SECTION 2 : HIGH SCHOOL ================= -->
  <section class="block" id="s2" aria-labelledby="s2-title">
    <div class="block-head">
      <span class="num" aria-hidden="true">2</span>
      <h2 id="s2-title">For a High Schooler <span class="zh-h">给高中生</span> <span class="emo">🎓</span></h2>
    </div>
    <div class="panels">
      <article class="panel en">
        <span class="chip en">EN · English</span>

        <h3>The short version</h3>
        <p class="lede">White sunlight is a mix of all visible wavelengths. As it passes through the atmosphere, air molecules (mostly <span class="m">N<sub>2</sub></span> and <span class="m">O<sub>2</sub></span>) <strong>scatter</strong> it — and they scatter short wavelengths way more than long ones. That scattered blue light is what you're seeing when you look up at the sky.</p>

        <h3>The physics</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>The nitrogen and oxygen molecules are about 0.3 nm across — tiny compared to the wavelength of visible light (400–700 nm).</li>
          <li>The oscillating electric field of the light shakes the molecules' electrons, which re-radiate light in all directions. This is called <strong>Rayleigh scattering</strong>.</li>
          <li>The amount of scattering drops off as the <strong>fourth power of wavelength</strong>:
            <div class="formula">scattering&nbsp;∝&nbsp;<span class="frac"><span class="top">1</span><span class="bot"><span class="m">λ<sup>4</sup></span></span></span></div>
          </li>
          <li>So blue light (450 nm) is scattered roughly <strong>(700/450)<sup>4</sup> ≈ 7–8×</strong> more than red light (700 nm). Look up and you see scattered blue; look at the sun and you see the direct light with the blue partly stripped out — slightly yellowish.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>“But wait — why not violet?”</h3>
        <p class="lede">Violet light has an even shorter wavelength and scatters even more, yet the sky doesn't look purple. Three reasons combine:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>The Sun emits less violet than blue (its spectrum fades toward the UV).</li>
          <li>Your eyes are far less sensitive to violet — your violet-sensitive (S) cones peak around 420–440 nm, and your overall brightness sensitivity at 400 nm is under 1%.</li>
          <li>The scattered violet + blue + a little green blend into the single color we call “blue” (~480 nm).</li>
        </ol>

        <h3>Bonus: sunsets</h3>
        <p class="lede">At sunset the light crosses the atmosphere at a shallow angle — through roughly <strong>10× more air</strong> than at noon (more like 30–40× near the horizon). The blue gets scattered out of the direct beam long before it reaches you, so what's left is the red end of the spectrum. That's a red sunset.</p>
        <p>Quick checks on your understanding:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Clouds are white</strong> because water droplets are huge compared to light (Mie scattering), which scatters all wavelengths about equally.</li>
          <li><strong>The Moon has no blue sky</strong> — no air to scatter anything.</li>
          <li><strong>Look at the sky 90° away from the Sun and put on polarized sunglasses</strong> — the blue darkens. Sky light is partially linearly polarized, a direct fingerprint of dipole scattering.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
      <article class="panel zh" lang="zh-Hans">
        <span class="chip zh">中文 · Simplified Chinese</span>

        <h3>简版</h3>
        <p class="lede">白色阳光是所有可见光波长的混合。当它穿过大气层时，空气分子（主要是 <span class="m">N<sub>2</sub></span> 和 <span class="m">O<sub>2</sub></span>）会把它<strong>散射</strong>开——而且短波长的光被散射得比长波长的多得多。你抬头看天空时看到的那些被散射的蓝光，就是这个原因。</p>

        <h3>物理原理</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>氮分子和氧分子的尺寸大约 0.3 纳米——与可见光的波长（400–700 纳米）相比小得可怜。</li>
          <li>光的振荡电场会搅动分子中的电子，电子再把光向四面八方重新辐射出去。这就叫<strong>瑞利散射</strong>。</li>
          <li>散射强度随波长的<strong>四次方</strong>反比衰减：
            <div class="formula">散射&nbsp;∝&nbsp;<span class="frac"><span class="top">1</span><span class="bot"><span class="m">λ<sup>4</sup></span></span></span></div>
          </li>
          <li>所以蓝光（450 纳米）被散射的量大约是红光（700 纳米）的 <strong>(700/450)<sup>4</sup> ≈ 7–8 倍</strong>。抬头看，你看到的是散射出来的蓝光；看太阳，你看到的是被剥掉了一部分蓝光的直射光——所以略带黄色。</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>“等等——那为什么不是紫色？”</h3>
        <p class="lede">紫光的波长更短，被散射得更厉害，可天空看起来却不是紫色。三个原因共同作用：</p>
        <ol>
          <li>太阳发出的紫光比蓝光少（它的光谱朝紫外方向逐渐减弱）。</li>
          <li>你的眼睛对紫光的灵敏度低得多——感知紫光的（S）视锥细胞在 420–440 纳米附近才达到峰值，而在 400 纳米处你的整体亮度灵敏度不到 1%。</li>
          <li>被散射的紫光 + 蓝光 + 一点绿光混合成了一种我们称之为“蓝色”的单一颜色（约 480 纳米）。</li>
        </ol>

        <h3>加餐：日落</h3>
        <p class="lede">日落时，阳光以很浅的角度穿过大气层——经过的空气比正午多大约 <strong>10 倍</strong>（靠近地平线时更像 30–40 倍）。蓝光早在到达你眼睛之前就被散射出直射光路了，所以剩下的就是光谱的红端。这就是红色日落的由来。</p>
        <p>快速检验一下你的理解：</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>云是白色的</strong>，因为水滴与光的波长相比大得多（米氏散射），它对所有波长的光散射得差不多一样多。</li>
          <li><strong>月球上没有蓝天</strong>——没有空气可以散射任何东西。</li>
          <li><strong>戴上偏振太阳镜，看离太阳 90° 方向的天空</strong>——蓝色会变暗。天光是部分线偏振光，这是偶极散射的直接“指纹”。</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ================= SECTION 3 : PhD ================= -->
  <section class="block" id="s3" aria-labelledby="s3-title">
    <div class="block-head">
      <span class="num" aria-hidden="true">3</span>
      <h2 id="s3-title">For a Physics PhD <span class="zh-h">给物理学博士</span> <span class="emo">🧪</span></h2>
    </div>
    <div class="panels">
      <article class="panel en">
        <span class="chip en">EN · English</span>

        <h3>3.1 The scattering formalism</h3>
        <p class="lede">In the Rayleigh regime (<span class="m">ka ≪ 1</span>, and here <span class="m">a ∼ 0.3</span> nm vs. <span class="m">λ = 400–700</span> nm, so <span class="m">ka ∼ 10<sup>−3</sup></span>), the incident field induces an electric dipole <span class="m">p = ε<sub>0</sub>αE</span>, and the radiated power gives a total scattering cross section</p>
        <div class="formula">
          <span class="m">σ<sub>R</sub>(λ)</span><span class="op">=</span>
          <span class="frac"><span class="top"><span class="m">k<sup>4</sup> α<sup>2</sup></span></span><span class="bot"><span class="m">6π ε<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup></span></span></span>
          <span class="op">·</span>
          <span class="frac"><span class="top"><span class="m">6 + 7ρ</span></span><span class="bot"><span class="m">6 (1 + ρ)</span></span></span>
        </div>
        <p>where <span class="m">α</span> is the molecular polarizability and <span class="m">ρ</span> the depolarization ratio (<span class="m">ρ<sub>air</sub> ≈ 0.02–0.03</span> from the anisotropic polarizability tensors of <span class="m">N<sub>2</sub></span> and <span class="m">O<sub>2</sub></span>; the correction is &lt;1%).</p>
        <p>Numbers, anchored at 550 nm:</p>
        <div class="tablewrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><span class="m">α(N<sub>2</sub>)</span></td><td>≈ 1.9 × 10<sup>−40</sup> F·m² (polarizability volume ≈ 1.7 Å³)</td></tr>
            <tr><td><span class="m">σ<sub>R</sub>(550 nm)</span></td><td>≈ 4 × 10<sup>−31</sup> m² per molecule</td></tr>
            <tr><td><span class="m">N</span> (sea level)</td><td>≈ 2.7 × 10<sup>25</sup> m<sup>−3</sup></td></tr>
            <tr><td><span class="m">β<sub>R</sub> = Nσ</span></td><td>≈ 1.2 × 10<sup>−5</sup> m<sup>−1</sup></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Vertical optical depth <span class="m">τ(550 nm)</span></td><td>≈ 0.1</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        </div>
        <p>The <span class="m">λ<sup>−4</sup></span> scaling (valid for non-resonant, quasi-static dipoles — it breaks down approaching electronic resonances, which is why the UV is more strongly scattered and why index dispersion corrections enter in the far UV) gives</p>
        <div class="formula">
          <span class="frac"><span class="top">β(400)</span><span class="bot">β(550)</span></span><span class="op">≈ 3.6,</span>
          <span class="frac"><span class="top">β(700)</span><span class="bot">β(550)</span></span><span class="op">≈ 0.38,</span>
          <span class="frac"><span class="top">β(400)</span><span class="bot">β(700)</span></span><span class="op">≈ 9.4</span>
        </div>
        <p>i.e. the sky's spectral distribution is blue-shifted by roughly a factor of 3–4 relative to the incident solar spectrum between the red and blue ends.</p>

        <h3>3.2 Why blue, not violet</h3>
        <p class="lede">Three independent factors act multiplicatively:</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Source spectrum.</strong> Top-of-atmosphere solar irradiance decreases monotonically through the visible (<span class="m">L(400 nm) ≈ 0.75 L(450 nm)</span>), with the <span class="m">O<sub>3</sub></span> Hartley band hard-cutting the UV.</li>
          <li><strong>Receiver sensitivity.</strong> The photopic luminosity function gives <span class="m">V(400 nm) ≲ 0.007</span>; S-cone sensitivity peaks at 420–440 nm. Violet scatters more, but it contributes little to the perceived chromaticity.</li>
          <li><strong>Colorimetry.</strong> The scattered spectrum is a smooth continuum over ~400–520 nm; its locus point falls in the medium-blue region (~480 nm).</li>
        </ol>
        <p>The sky is thus not a spectral color — “blue” here is a perceptual mixture, and the violet is present (measure the sky's spectrum: it does extend past 400 nm, bluer than the solar spectrum).</p>

        <h3>3.3 Sunset and sunrise, quantitatively</h3>
        <p class="lede">With geometric air mass <span class="m">X ≈ 1/sin h</span> (refraction-corrected, <span class="m">X ≈ 38</span> at the horizon):</p>
        <ul>
          <li><span class="m">τ(400 nm, h → 0) ≈ 0.36 × 38 ≈ 14 ⇒ e<sup>−τ</sup> ∼ 8 × 10<sup>−7</sup></span> — the blue is essentially extinct from the direct beam.</li>
          <li><span class="m">τ(700 nm, h → 0) ≈ 0.038 × 38 ≈ 1.45 ⇒ e<sup>−τ</sup> ≈ 0.23</span> — red survives, depleted but present.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The sunset color is then tuned by:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Aerosols</strong> (Mie, weakly <span class="m">λ</span>-dependent): make the disk deeper red and wash out the blue sky; this is why a hazy noon is pale gray-white.</li>
          <li><strong>Molecular absorption</strong>: <span class="m">O<sub>3</sub></span> Chappuis band (560–750 nm), <span class="m">O<sub>2</sub>–O<sub>2</sub></span> collision-induced absorption near 687 nm, <span class="m">CH<sub>4</sub></span> at 720 nm — these carve the red into crimson/purple.</li>
          <li><strong>Refractive dispersion</strong>: the green flash is the 532–550 nm window surviving the chromatic shear at the very last degrees of setting.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>3.4 Polarization — the clean experimental signature</h3>
        <p class="lede">Dipole radiation has angular pattern <span class="m">I(θ) ∝ 1 + cos<sup>2</sup>θ</span>, so single-scattered skylight at scattering angle <span class="m">θ</span> is linearly polarized in the plane of the sky with degree</p>
        <div class="formula">
          <span class="m">P(θ)</span><span class="op">=</span>
          <span class="frac"><span class="top"><span class="m">sin<sup>2</sup>θ</span></span><span class="bot"><span class="m">1 + cos<sup>2</sup>θ</span></span></span>
          <span class="op">→ 1</span><span class="op">(θ = 90°)</span>
        </div>
        <p>Ideal single scattering predicts 100% at 90°; the measured zenith-sky maximum is ~75%, reduced by multiple scattering, the small depolarization ratio, and ground/horizon reflections. Babinet's polarimeter on the 1800s sky is a textbook confirmation of the dipole model.</p>

        <h3>3.5 Cross-checks on other worlds</h3>
        <div class="tablewrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>World</th><th>Atmosphere</th><th>Sky color</th><th>Diagnosis</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Earth</td><td><span class="m">N<sub>2</sub>/O<sub>2</sub></span></td><td>Blue (day), red (sunset)</td><td>Rayleigh-dominated</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Mars</td><td><span class="m">CO<sub>2</sub></span> + dust</td><td>Butterscotch (day), <strong>blue at sunset</strong></td><td>Rayleigh (<span class="m">CO<sub>2</sub></span>) + Mie dust; dust dominates the disk, gas dominates the anti-solar horizon</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Titan</td><td><span class="m">N<sub>2</sub></span> + tholin haze</td><td>Orange, hazy</td><td>Aerosol Mie overwhelms Rayleigh</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Moon</td><td>None</td><td>Black, always</td><td>No scatterer, no sky</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        </div>
        <p>The blue sky is therefore not a generic property of atmospheres — it requires <em>Rayleigh scattering by molecules to dominate the optical depth in the visible</em>, with the Sun's spectrum and the observer's eye doing the “not-violet” selection.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="panel zh" lang="zh-Hans">
        <span class="chip zh">中文 · Simplified Chinese</span>

        <h3>3.1 散射的形式化描述</h3>
        <p class="lede">在瑞利散射区域（<span class="m">ka ≪ 1</span>；这里 <span class="m">a ∼ 0.3</span> 纳米，而 <span class="m">λ = 400–700</span> 纳米，故 <span class="m">ka ∼ 10<sup>−3</sup></span>），入射场诱导出一个电偶极子 <span class="m">p = ε<sub>0</sub>αE</span>，其辐射功率给出总散射截面</p>
        <div class="formula">
          <span class="m">σ<sub>R</sub>(λ)</span><span class="op">=</span>
          <span class="frac"><span class="top"><span class="m">k<sup>4</sup> α<sup>2</sup></span></span><span class="bot"><span class="m">6π ε<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup></span></span></span>
          <span class="op">·</span>
          <span class="frac"><span class="top"><span class="m">6 + 7ρ</span></span><span class="bot"><span class="m">6 (1 + ρ)</span></span></span>
        </div>
        <p>其中 <span class="m">α</span> 是分子极化率，<span class="m">ρ</span> 是退偏比（<span class="m">ρ<sub>air</sub> ≈ 0.02–0.03</span>，来自 <span class="m">N<sub>2</sub></span> 和 <span class="m">O<sub>2</sub></span> 的各向异性极化率张量；修正量 &lt;1%）。</p>
        <p>以 550 纳米为基准的数值：</p>
        <div class="tablewrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>物理量</th><th>数值</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><span class="m">α(N<sub>2</sub>)</span></td><td>≈ 1.9 × 10<sup>−40</sup> F·m²（极化率体积 ≈ 1.7 Å³）</td></tr>
            <tr><td><span class="m">σ<sub>R</sub>(550 nm)</span></td><td>≈ 4 × 10<sup>−31</sup> m²（每个分子）</td></tr>
            <tr><td><span class="m">N</span>（海平面）</td><td>≈ 2.7 × 10<sup>25</sup> m<sup>−3</sup></td></tr>
            <tr><td><span class="m">β<sub>R</sub> = Nσ</span></td><td>≈ 1.2 × 10<sup>−5</sup> m<sup>−1</sup></td></tr>
            <tr><td>垂直光学厚度 <span class="m">τ(550 nm)</span></td><td>≈ 0.1</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        </div>
        <p><span class="m">λ<sup>−4</sup></span> 标度律（适用于非共振、准静态偶极子——接近电子共振时会失效，这也是紫外光被散射得更强的原因，并且远紫外区需要引入折射率色散修正）给出</p>
        <div class="formula">
          <span class="frac"><span class="top">β(400)</span><span class="bot">β(550)</span></span><span class="op">≈ 3.6,</span>
          <span class="frac"><span class="top">β(700)</span><span class="bot">β(550)</span></span><span class="op">≈ 0.38,</span>
          <span class="frac"><span class="top">β(400)</span><span class="bot">β(700)</span></span><span class="op">≈ 9.4</span>
        </div>
        <p>也就是说，天光的能谱分布相对于入射太阳光谱，在蓝端与红端之间大约蓝移了 3–4 倍。</p>

        <h3>3.2 为什么是蓝色而不是紫色</h3>
        <p class="lede">三个相互独立的因素相乘作用：</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>光源谱。</strong>大气层顶的太阳辐照度在可见光范围内单调递减（<span class="m">L(400 nm) ≈ 0.75 L(450 nm)</span>），<span class="m">O<sub>3</sub></span> 的哈特利带则硬切掉了紫外波段。</li>
          <li><strong>接收器灵敏度。</strong>明视觉光度函数给出 <span class="m">V(400 nm) ≲ 0.007</span>；S 视锥细胞的灵敏度在 420–440 纳米处达到峰值。紫光虽然散射得更多，但对所感知色度的贡献很小。</li>
          <li><strong>色度学。</strong>散射光谱在 ~400–520 纳米范围内是平滑的连续谱；其色度轨迹点落在中蓝区域（~480 纳米）。</li>
        </ol>
        <p>因此天空并不是单色（光谱）色——这里的“蓝色”是一种感知上的混合，而且紫光确实存在（去测量天空的光谱：它确实延伸到 400 纳米之外，比太阳光谱更偏蓝）。</p>

        <h3>3.3 定量地看日落与日出</h3>
        <p class="lede">取几何大气质量 <span class="m">X ≈ 1/sin h</span>（经折射修正后，地平线处 <span class="m">X ≈ 38</span>）：</p>
        <ul>
          <li><span class="m">τ(400 nm, h → 0) ≈ 0.36 × 38 ≈ 14 ⇒ e<sup>−τ</sup> ∼ 8 × 10<sup>−7</sup></span> —— 蓝光在直射光路中基本被完全衰减。</li>
          <li><span class="m">τ(700 nm, h → 0) ≈ 0.038 × 38 ≈ 1.45 ⇒ e<sup>−τ</sup> ≈ 0.23</span> —— 红光幸存，虽然被削弱但依然存在。</li>
        </ul>
        <p>日落的颜色随后由以下因素调节：</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>气溶胶</strong>（米氏散射，对 <span class="m">λ</span> 依赖弱）：让太阳圆面变得更深红，并把蓝天冲淡；这就是为什么雾蒙蒙的正午呈灰白色。</li>
          <li><strong>分子吸收</strong>：<span class="m">O<sub>3</sub></span> 查普伊斯带（560–750 纳米）、<span class="m">O<sub>2</sub>–O<sub>2</sub></span> 碰撞诱导吸收（687 纳米附近）、<span class="m">CH<sub>4</sub></span> 在 720 纳米——这些把红色雕凿成绯红/紫色。</li>
          <li><strong>折射色散</strong>：绿闪光是 532–550 纳米窗口在落下最后几度时经受色散剪切后幸存下来的部分。</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>3.4 偏振——干净的实验特征</h3>
        <p class="lede">偶极辐射的角分布为 <span class="m">I(θ) ∝ 1 + cos<sup>2</sup>θ</span>，因此在散射角 <span class="m">θ</span> 处的单次散射天光在天空平面内呈线偏振，其偏振度为</p>
        <div class="formula">
          <span class="m">P(θ)</span><span class="op">=</span>
          <span class="frac"><span class="top"><span class="m">sin<sup>2</sup>θ</span></span><span class="bot"><span class="m">1 + cos<sup>2</sup>θ</span></span></span>
          <span class="op">→ 1</span><span class="op">(θ = 90°)</span>
        </div>
        <p>理想单次散射在 90° 处预言 100% 偏振；实测天顶方向的最大值约为 75%，被多次散射、较小的退偏比以及地面/地平线反射所削弱。19 世纪巴比涅（Babinet）用偏振计对天空所做的测量，是对偶极模型教科书式的证实。</p>

        <h3>3.5 其他天体的对照验证</h3>
        <div class="tablewrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>世界</th><th>大气</th><th>天空颜色</th><th>诊断</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>地球</td><td><span class="m">N<sub>2</sub>/O<sub>2</sub></span></td><td>白天蓝色，日落时红色</td><td>瑞利散射主导</td></tr>
            <tr><td>火星</td><td><span class="m">CO<sub>2</sub></span> + 尘埃</td><td>白天焦糖色，<strong>日落时蓝色</strong></td><td>瑞利散射（<span class="m">CO<sub>2</sub></span>）+ 米氏尘埃；尘埃主导太阳圆面方向，气体主导反太阳方向的地平线</td></tr>
            <tr><td>土卫六</td><td><span class="m">N<sub>2</sub></span> + 托林雾</td><td>橙色、朦胧</td><td>气溶胶的米氏散射压倒瑞利散射</td></tr>
            <tr><td>月球</td><td>无</td><td>永远是黑色</td><td>没有散射体，就没有天空</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        </div>
        <p>因此，蓝天并不是大气的普遍属性——它要求<em>分子的瑞利散射在可见光波段主导光学厚度</em>，再由太阳光谱和观察者眼睛完成“不是紫色”的选择。</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</main>

