The user is asking for a theme-switching button in index.html:
- "Day mode" (light blue) — the current theme
- "Night mode" (dark starry sky) — a dark theme with stars
- Smooth CSS transitions

Plan:
1. Add CSS custom properties for both themes. Define the current colors under `:root` (day), and override them with a `data-theme="night"` (or `body.night`) selector.
2. Smooth transitions: add `transition` on background-color, color, border-color, etc. for relevant elements. A global `* { transition: background-color .4s, ... }` can be tricky, but it's a common technique. Better: add transitions on specific elements (body, .panel, .topbar, th, td, .formula, .chip, .topbar nav a, etc.). Or use a `html.theme-anim *` approach. Let me do targeted transitions on elements that have theme-dependent colors, plus a short universal transition scoped to a "theming" class during theme changes to avoid interfering with other transitions (like the .match box-shadow or the nav a transitions). Actually the simplest and most robust approach is: add a `body.theming *, body.theming *::before, body.theming *::after { transition: background-color .45s ease, color .45s ease, border-color .45s ease, fill .45s ease !important; }` class that JS adds for the duration of the switch. Hmm, but with !important it would override the .match transition — during the 450ms the match hover wouldn't animate, but that's a trivial issue.

   A cleaner alternative approach: explicitly define transitions on all theme-dependent elements. There are a lot of them (body, .topbar, .panel, .chip.en/.zh, th, td, tbody tr:hover, .formula, .block-head .num?, footer, #toTop, nav a). Doable, but the universal "theming" class approach is more maintainable and reliably smooth. Let me combine: use a `theming` class toggled for 500ms.

   Actually, a simpler and pretty standard approach: put transitions directly on the elements that get recolored. Let me enumerate the elements with theme-dependent colors:
   - body (background, color)
   - #progress (background gradient — transitions on background-image don't animate; acceptable, the bar is tiny)
   - .topbar (background, border-bottom color)
   - .topbar nav a (color, background on hover/active, border)
   - .controls button (background, border, color)
   - main? no
   - .num (background is a fixed gradient — leave as-is in both themes, it's an accent)
   - .block-head .zh-h (color var(--accent) — transitions if color has a transition)
   - .panel (background, border, color, shadow)
   - .chip.en/.zh (background, color)
   - .panel h3 (color)
   - .panel p em (muted color)
   - .panel .formula (background gradient — can't smoothly animate gradients; in night mode I could use a single dark background color instead of a gradient so it transitions smoothly. Or use a color that transitions. Let me make .formula background a solid color variable (no gradient) so it transitions smoothly.)
   - .panel zh .formula (solid color variable)
   - th (background, color, border)
   - td (border color)
   - tbody tr:hover (background)
   - footer (border-top, color)
   - #toTop (background, border, color)
   - .intro (color)
   - .brand .zh (muted)

   That's a lot. The "theming" universal transition class is cleaner. Let me use:

```css
body.theming, body.theming *{
  transition:background-color .45s ease,color .45s ease,border-color .45s ease,box-shadow .45s ease !important;
}
```

Wait — applying to * during theming means the .match box-shadow and nav hover will also transition (no problem, just adds smoothness), and the reveal transform transitions aren't affected (transform isn't in the list) — good. The `!important` overrides existing transition properties on those elements during theming, which is acceptable.

JS: on toggle, add 'theming' to body, swap the theme attribute, and remove 'theming' after 500ms.

3. Starry sky for night mode: generate stars with CSS. Approach:
   - Use multiple radial-gradient backgrounds on body::before or a fixed pseudo-element for tiny star dots, plus a few "twinkling" star layers via box-shadow. Classic technique: a fixed div `#stars` with `background-image: radial-gradient(1px 1px at ...)` repeated. A single element with `background-repeat: repeat` and multiple `radial-gradient` star layers at different positions/sizes — gives a tiled starry sky. To add depth, two star layers of different tile sizes.
   - Twinkle: animate opacity of layers with a keyframe (subtle), respecting prefers-reduced-motion.
   - In day mode, the star layer is hidden (opacity 0); transition to opacity 1 in night mode.

   Implementation:
```css
.stars{
  position:fixed;inset:0;z-index:-1;pointer-events:none;
  opacity:0;transition:opacity .8s ease;
}
body[data-theme="night"] .stars{opacity:1}
